# What git tracks here: the scripts, the documents, and the one file a person
# made by hand. Everything else is either downloaded from the General Court or
# generated from what was, and can be rebuilt.
#
# The working folder is about 8 GB. Nearly all of it is caption files. Git is
# here to make code changes reversible and to satisfy the desktop app, not to
# archive data -- a repository carrying work/ would be unusable.

# ---- captions and recordings: 7.6 GB, re-fetchable -----------------------
work/
*.mp4
*.m4a
*.wav

# ---- the built site: regenerated by build_all.py in 20 seconds -----------
site/
.wrangler/

# ---- fetched page caches: re-fetchable, and large -----------------------
bill_pages/
bill_text/
rc_pages/
status_pages/
calendars/
calendars_senate/
member_pages/
nh-archive/

# ---- bulk downloads from the General Court -------------------------------
# Re-downloaded in one request each. They change on every fetch, so tracking
# them would store a new copy of a 4.7 MB file every day for no benefit.
Docket.txt
LSRs.txt
LsrsOnly.txt
LsrSponsors.txt
RollCallSummary.txt
RollCallHistory.txt
legislators.txt
Members.txt
HouseDistricts.txt
Counties.txt
Committees.txt
SubjectCodes.txt

# ---- generated data ------------------------------------------------------
# Every one of these is produced by a build_* step from the files above.
data/
.build.lock
narratives.json
topics_assigned.json
bill_status.json
committee_reports.json
chapters.json
senate_reports.json
testimony_db.json
archive_bills.json
floor_index.json
journals.json
calendars.json
former_members.json
rollcalls.json
bill_text.json
amendments.json
# One per term now: build_manifest.py writes verification_manifest.csv for
# the current term and verification_manifest_<term>.csv for each archived
# one, and build_proceedings.py reads them all. Named individually, the
# per-term files were not matched and one was committed.
verification_manifest*.csv
verification_manifest*.xlsx
proceedings.csv
candidate_segments.json
_captions_manifest.csv

# ---- caches, lists and logs ----------------------------------------------
.segment_cache.json
missing_captions.txt
no_captions.txt
logs/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/

# ---- generated documents -------------------------------------------------
# STATE.md is written by handoff.py. Committing it would put a stale copy in
# the repository for someone to trust.
STATE.md
testimony.json
testimony_probe.json
floor_H_2026-03-05.json
probe_billtext.html
missing_phrases.txt
check.txt

# ==========================================================================
# Tracked on purpose, against the patterns above.
#
# ground_truth.csv is 35 proceedings a person timed by watching the video. It
# is the only measurement of this system that was not generated, every
# timestamp method is scored against it, and it has already been lost twice to
# a rebuild. It is a .csv sitting among generated .csv files, so it is spelled
# out here rather than left to a pattern.
!ground_truth.csv
!status/officials.txt
!versions.json

# rollcalls/ is tracked, and the bulk downloads above are not, for the reason
# given there: those change on every fetch. A past session's roll calls do not.
# The General Court publishes one bulk file per CURRENT session, so 2025's
# 380 votes and 107,118 member votes cannot be downloaded at all -- they exist
# here only because fetch_rollcalls_db.py read them out of the database, and a
# git clean would cost another round trip to a server this project does not
# control. 3.8 MB, written once per session year and never rewritten.

# ---- probe output: a snapshot of one page, re-fetchable in one request ---
probe_calendars*.html

# ---- probe output: page snapshots, re-fetchable in one request --------
committees_*.html
probe_db*.json
probe_legacy_*.html
archive_samples/
archive_sample_ids.json
committees.json
docket_pages/
Docket_*.txt

# Patch scripts are copied in from the scratchpad, run, and deleted. One
# survived a failed run and was committed.
_*.py

# ---- the database, dumped whole by fetch_archive_db.py -------------------
# 2.8 million rows and about 400 MB. Re-fetchable in one run, and it changes
# whenever the General Court updates it.
db/

# ---- parsed out of the calendars by calendar_meetings.py ----------------
# 21,611 rows, 6 MB, rebuilt from calendars/ in about a minute with no network.
meetings.json

# ---- the House meeting schedule, fetched by fetch_schedule.py -----------
schedule_pages/
schedule.json
archive_years.json
archive/
journals/
journals_senate/

# The bench's sample pools are derived and rebuilt by review.py
# --refresh. review/checked.jsonl is NOT ignored: it is the record a
# person made, and it belongs in git beside ground_truth.csv.
review/.pool-*.json

# ---- credentials ---------------------------------------------------------
# The only secret this repository has is a YouTube Data API key, and
# secrets.example.json shows the shape without carrying one. This repository
# is meant to be shared: a key committed once lives in the history forever,
# and preflight fails if a tracked file starts to look like it holds one.
#
# probe_db.py's database credentials are NOT secret and stay in the open --
# the General Court publishes them at gc.nh.gov/downloads for public use.
secrets.json
.env
*.pem
*.key
!secrets.example.json

legislation/

# The sponsor sweep: 2,614 members x 2 answers, saved gzipped so the parser
# can be wrong without costing a request. Generated, and large.
sponsors_pages/
sponsors_members.json
sponsors_form.html

# The General Court's own list of past and present legislators, one request.
past_members.json

# The roll call detail pages, fetched for the party they carry.
rollcall_party_pages/
member_party.json
